@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ Extension discovery uses one Hayai-owned `ContentKind` policy for browse, search
 
 APK repository URLs remain in J2K's single `extensionRepos` preference. Fresh installations start with the user-selected Keiyoushi and Cursed Manga protobuf indexes; both remain Manga repositories, with adult visibility still controlled by J2K's source filter. Hayai stores novel membership tags only for backup, restore, and redirect compatibility. The APK repository section lists every global APK index, regardless of content kind; adding, removing, or replacing a URL updates the global set and Hayai tags together. Browse and Browse settings expose explicit APK-index and LNReader JavaScript destinations, but both destinations reuse J2K's `RepoController`, inline create row, adapter, holder, validation spinner, edit, delete, and undo behavior. `RepoPresenter` switches only the persistence and validation backend. A JavaScript repository's display name is derived from its URL after its index validates, so adding either repository format requires exactly one URL field and no separate form. Fresh installations seed the supplied LNReader v3 index once; removing it is durable and does not recreate it on restart.
 
-LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. `NovelPluginRepositoryDocument` normalizes current root arrays and legacy `plugins` or `sources` envelopes, removes invalid optional catalogue websites, and percent-encodes raw path brackets before URI validation. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. Android application context enters explicitly through `NovelPluginManager`, `NovelPluginSource`, `NovelPluginRuntime`, and `NovelPluginLibrary`; the runtime never resolves an unregistered raw `Context` from global DI. Browse rows resolve relative plugin icon paths against the trusted repository and load them through the same Coil-backed source image view used by J2K. Source construction and asynchronous warm-up failures remain attached to the current installed plugin ID in the extension catalog instead of disappearing. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs.
+LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. `NovelPluginRepositoryDocument` normalizes current root arrays and legacy `plugins` or `sources` envelopes, removes invalid optional catalogue websites, and percent-encodes raw path brackets before URI validation. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. Android application context enters explicitly through `NovelPluginManager`, `NovelPluginSource`, `NovelPluginRuntime`, and `NovelPluginLibrary`; the runtime never resolves an unregistered raw `Context` from global DI. Browse rows resolve relative plugin icon paths against the trusted repository and load them through the same Coil-backed source image view used by J2K. Source construction and asynchronous warm-up failures remain attached to the current installed plugin ID in the extension catalog instead of disappearing. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs. `NovelPluginManager` restores a missing trust record for every repository that the user already configured. This repairs repositories migrated from builds that persisted the repository before its trust record. Existing signed trust records remain unchanged.
 
 ## Database reset and migration
 
@@ -54,7 +54,7 @@ The text reader owns its lifecycle behind a Hayai renderer contract. Its XML she
 
 Read-aloud state lives in a Hayai foreground media-playback service rather than the activity. The notification exposes play, pause, stop, and paragraph navigation, and a recreated reader can attach to the active service. Quotes support duplicate-safe creation, editing, attributed copying, ordering, and deletion. Native selection actions route quote creation back into the existing quote dialog. Definition and search actions open a forced resizable Custom Tab so the user's browser session remains available. Google Translate first tries its process-text and send contracts, then uses the same browser path. A secure `E2EBottomSheetDialog` WebView preserves only WebView cookies as the fallback and blocks navigation outside HTTPS. Full-chapter translation and cached in-app selection translation remain separate typed actions. Translation providers, dictionary handoff, highlight recovery, visual custom-source compilation, statistics, offline chapters, presets, snippets, and EPUB export do not change the image reader. Both renderers present bounded chapter blocks with stable IDs, append and prepend without changing focus, preserve the visible offset, and move J2K progress when a new block becomes visible. Emulator verification remains required before this implementation becomes a verified port.
 
-Novel integration uses a small set of presentation adapters rather than parallel J2K models. The library filter delegates content-kind classification to Hayai. Manga details decorate the existing chapter rows with percent, word count, reading time, and verified offline state; the normal download/remove controls delegate novel chapters to `NovelOfflineManager`. J2K statistics aggregate Hayai's derived word data, shortcuts preserve novel identity, and migration searches reject novel-to-image and image-to-novel targets. `NovelSettingsController` owns the complete text-reader preference surface, typed reusable presets, CSS/JavaScript snippets, and bounded text-replacement rules. Translation, dictionary, and visual-source-builder settings use J2K `SettingsController` screens and preference rows while their drafts, validation, network preview, and persistence remain Hayai-owned. J2K remains authoritative for chapters, progress, history, categories, and the enclosing screens.
+Novel integration uses a small set of adapters rather than parallel J2K models. The library filter delegates content-kind classification to Hayai. Manga details decorate the existing chapter rows with percent, word count, reading time, and verified offline state. Novel chapter downloads enter J2K's `DownloadQueue`, `DownloadJob`, status flow, notification, details actions, and Downloads sheet. `NovelDownloadQueueSource` supplies the queue-only `HttpSource` shape that J2K's `Download` model requires. The adapter retains the real source ID, name, language, novel marker, and metered policy. No HTTP operation runs through the adapter. `Downloader` calls `NovelDownloadDelegate` only to check and save a novel chapter in Hayai's document store. `DownloadStore` reconstructs the same adapter after process restart, and `Downloader` drops a restored item when the atomic novel document already exists. The details adapter preserves J2K's active `QUEUE`, `DOWNLOADING`, and `ERROR` states when it refreshes offline novel metadata. The normal queue listener marks the offline row before J2K removes a successful queue item. Image sources continue through J2K's original directory and page path without an adapter. Novel removal deletes the Hayai document and refreshes the same details row. J2K statistics aggregate Hayai's derived word data, shortcuts preserve novel identity, and migration searches reject novel-to-image and image-to-novel targets. `NovelSettingsController` owns the complete text-reader preference surface, typed reusable presets, CSS and JavaScript snippets, and bounded text-replacement rules. Translation, dictionary, and visual-source-builder settings use J2K `SettingsController` screens and preference rows while their drafts, validation, network preview, and persistence remain Hayai-owned. J2K remains authoritative for chapters, download scheduling, progress, history, categories, and the enclosing screens.
 
 The novel reader inflates J2K's existing `reader_activity` and binds its existing app bar, toolbar, viewer container, navigation controls, chapter sheet, and slider. Hayai applies the same system-bar and display-cutout margins as J2K at that shared layout boundary. Reader chrome remains an overlay; novel content uses one constant safe inset equal to the ignored status-bar inset plus J2K's `actionBarSize`. Showing or hiding chrome therefore neither obscures nor reflows text. The image-reader activity remains unchanged.
 
@@ -66,6 +66,8 @@ Built-in source settings are another deliberate adapter seam. `BrowseSourceContr
 
 J2K's extension-facing `JavaScriptEngine` delegates evaluation to Hayai's QuickJS runtime. The novel plugin bridge needs the Dokar binding API, while J2K previously packaged a second QuickJS implementation with the same native `libquickjs.so` name. One Hayai-owned evaluator preserves the extension API and prevents an unresolvable APK native-library collision without changing application lifecycle code.
 
+The Cloudflare interceptor follows Mihon commit `ac249e3668a57f24782a49218834064459ba2d09`. It detects the official `cf-mitigated: challenge` response header and stops the bypass when Cloudflare reports an interactive challenge. Hayai keeps J2K's interceptor contract, cookies, user agent, strings, and call sites unchanged.
+
 Source badges and bundled icons use Hayai-owned presentation resolvers. The browse list and both migration lists render only facts that the live source proves: `Bundled`, `JS`, `Novel`, and `Adult`. The resolver does not label an enhanced-source family until its delegated behavior exists. The J2K `Source.icon()` seam asks Injekt for the registered `Application`, not an unregistered raw `Context`, before resolving a bundled fallback. This keeps the badge UI honest and source rows safe while limiting J2K changes to presentation holders and one icon fallback.
 
 Hayai-owned interface text uses `hayai_` Android resources. Pure parsers and services return typed labels or failure reasons when their output reaches the interface. Android-aware adapters resolve those types without translating source titles, protocol values, selectors, URLs, or server data. J2K presentation seams receive a `Context` only when they render a Hayai badge. `tools/verify-localization.ps1` rejects new interface literals, validates resource placeholders, and records reviewed nonlocalizable literals separately from the pinned J2K exception list.
